  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Lamp-Lit Response Sheet",
    "overview": "The product is a person at a desk after dark, filling in an OMR practice booklet by lamp light. The room recedes into ink; the page in front of them is warm, ruled, and finite. Every screen is a sheet of paper on that desk, and every record is a line on the sheet: a numbered row, a bubble to fill, a printed section header with a rule under it, a margin note in graphite.\n\nThe system is deliberately un-digital. It rejects the growth-dashboard conventions: no gradients, no glass, no soft corner radii, no colored side borders, no accent eyebrow above every heading. Hierarchy comes from print grammar — masthead, numbered sections, hairlines, highlighter marks, rubber stamps. State changes are committed like ink: a bubble fills instantly, a highlighter swipe crosses the row once, and the page stays still.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two materials only: ink room and paper sheet.",
      "Hairline rules and printed section headers, never cards floating on a gradient.",
      "A single highlighter accent (#d99a2b) plus two stamps (red, green).",
      "Archivo for voice, Roboto Mono for data.",
      "Motion is reserved for marking a line done; everything else is instant."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Register Rule",
        "body": "Text is bone on ink or graphite on paper. Never invert a single word inside a sentence for emphasis — use weight, not color.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Rationed Highlighter Rule",
        "body": "The highlighter is the highest-value ink on the page. It appears on the current item, the current day, and the mark of completion. If two things on a screen both want it, one of them is wrong.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "Any number that appears in a column, a count, or a progress readout uses Roboto Mono with tabular numerals. Proportional figures in a table are a defect.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Label-Not-Eyebrow Rule",
        "body": "Uppercase tracked labels belong to form fields and section indices, sitting beside or above their own value. A tracked accent line above a heading, used as decoration, is prohibited.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Ink, Lit Paper Rule",
        "body": "There is one elevation event: a sheet lifted off the ink. Everything else is flat, and depth is the material change. Shadows always carry an offset and a blur.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep one sheet per screen and separate sections with hairlines and numbered headers.",
